lesson: 115
title: For every few of one, so many of another
stars: 0
explain: «For every 2 red beads there are 3 blue beads» means the beads come in groups: each group has 2 red and 3 blue.
explain: Find how many groups there are from the colour you know, then multiply for the other colour.
explain: With 10 red beads there are 10 / 2 = 5 groups, so there are 5 × 3 = 15 blue beads.
vars: a=name p=2..5 q=2..6 m=2..8
need: p != q
answer: q * m
exercise: For every {p} red beads on the string there are {q} blue beads. There are {p*m} red beads. How many blue beads are there?
exercise: In a garden, for every {p} roses there are {q} tulips. There are {p*m} roses. How many tulips are there?
exercise: {a} mixes {q} cups of water with every {p} cups of juice. {a.He} uses {p*m} cups of juice. How many cups of water does {a.he} use?
exercise: In a class there are {q} girls for every {p} boys. There are {p*m} boys. How many girls are there?
exercise: A recipe uses {q} cups of milk for every {p} cups of flour. How many cups of milk go with {p*m} cups of flour?
exercise: On a farm there are {q} ducks for every {p} hens. The farm has {p*m} hens. How many ducks does it have?
test: The paint is made of {p} parts yellow and {q} parts blue. {a} has {p*m} cans of yellow. How many cans of blue does {a.he} need?
test: How many green tiles are on the wall if it has {p*m} white tiles and there are always {q} green tiles for each {p} white ones?
test: {a} plants seeds in the pattern {p} carrots, then {q} beans, again and again. When {a.he} has planted {p*m} carrots, how many beans has {a.he} planted?
test: There are {p*m} cars in the car park. For each {p} cars there are {q} bikes. How many bikes are there?
exercise: There are {q} blue beads for every {p} red beads. With {p*m} red beads, how many blue beads are there?
exercise: A farm in Iowa has {q} cows for every {p} horses. There are {p*m} horses. How many cows are there?
exercise: For each {p} cups of rice {a} adds {q} cups of water. How many cups of water go with {p*m} cups of rice?
exercise: Boys and girls in a club are in the ratio {p} to {q}. There are {p*m} boys. How many girls are there?
exercise: In a park in Sydney there are {q} gum trees for every {p} pine trees. If there are {p*m} pine trees, how many gum trees are there?
exercise: {a} in Ireland knits {q} red rows after every {p} green rows. When {a.he} has knitted {p*m} green rows, how many red rows has {a.he} knitted?
exercise: A recipe needs {q} spoons of sugar for every {p} spoons of cocoa. How many spoons of sugar are needed for {p*m} spoons of cocoa?
exercise: On a bookshelf in Leeds there are {q} storybooks per {p} comics. There are {p*m} comics. How many storybooks are there?
test: How many oranges go into a fruit punch with {p*m} lemons, if the punch always has {q} oranges for each {p} lemons?
test: {a} from Montreal fills an album: every time {a.he} puts in {p} star stickers, {a.he} adds {q} heart stickers. There are {p*m} star stickers in it now. How many heart stickers are there?
test: A school bus in Ohio carries {p*m} children. For every {p} children there are {q} bags. How many bags are on the bus?
test: The ratio of white sheep to black sheep in a field in New Zealand is {p} : {q}. If there are {p*m} white sheep, how many black sheep are there?
test: A necklace repeats {p} pearls and then {q} shells. It has {p*m} pearls. How many shells does it have?
test: There are {p*m} chairs in a hall, and {q} cushions go with each group of {p} chairs. How many cushions are there?
